✅ QUESTION 5: FIND ARTICULATION POINTS IN A GRAPH (TARJAN’S ALGORITHM)

This is advanced graph theory and a huge plus in interviews.
If you explain this well, interviewers know you’re solid.

🔹 Problem Statement

You are given an undirected connected graph with V vertices.

An Articulation Point (Cut Vertex) is a vertex such that removing it (and its edges) increases the number of connected components of the graph.

Your task is to find all articulation points.

✏️ Example
Graph:
0 —— 1 —— 3
 \   |
   \ |
     2


Articulation Points:

1


Removing node 1 disconnects the graph.

🔹 Key Concepts You MUST Know
Term	Meaning
disc[u]	Discovery time of node u in DFS
low[u]	Lowest discovery time reachable from u
parent[u]	Parent of u in DFS tree
🔹 Tarjan’s Conditions
Case 1: Root Node

If root has more than one child → articulation point

Case 2: Non-root Node

If:

low[v] ≥ disc[u]


Then u is an articulation point.

🔹 Algorithm (Step-by-Step)

Perform DFS

Assign discovery time and low time

Track parent and children count

Apply articulation conditions

⏱ Time Complexity: O(V + E)
📦 Space Complexity: O(V)

🔹 Java Code
import java.util.*;

class Solution {

    private int time = 0;

    public void articulationPoints(int V, List<List<Integer>> adj) {
        boolean[] visited = new boolean[V];
        int[] disc = new int[V];
        int[] low = new int[V];
        int[] parent = new int[V];
        boolean[] ap = new boolean[V];

        Arrays.fill(parent, -1);

        for (int i = 0; i < V; i++) {
            if (!visited[i]) {
                dfs(i, visited, disc, low, parent, ap, adj);
            }
        }

        for (int i = 0; i < V; i++) {
            if (ap[i]) {
                System.out.print(i + " ");
            }
        }
    }

    private void dfs(int u, boolean[] visited, int[] disc, int[] low,
                     int[] parent, boolean[] ap, List<List<Integer>> adj) {

        visited[u] = true;
        disc[u] = low[u] = ++time;
        int children = 0;

        for (int v : adj.get(u)) {

            if (!visited[v]) {
                children++;
                parent[v] = u;
                dfs(v, visited, disc, low, parent, ap, adj);

                low[u] = Math.min(low[u], low[v]);

                // Case 1: root node
                if (parent[u] == -1 && children > 1) {
                    ap[u] = true;
                }

                // Case 2: non-root node
                if (parent[u] != -1 && low[v] >= disc[u]) {
                    ap[u] = true;
                }

            } else if (v != parent[u]) {
                low[u] = Math.min(low[u], disc[v]);
            }
        }
    }
}

🔹 Line-by-Line Explanation
disc[u] = low[u] = ++time;


Assign discovery and low time.

low[u] = Math.min(low[u], low[v]);


Update low time using child’s information.

if (parent[u] == -1 && children > 1)


Root articulation condition.

if (parent[u] != -1 && low[v] >= disc[u])


Non-root articulation condition.

🔹 Dry Run Insight

If a child v cannot reach any ancestor of u, then removing u disconnects v.

That’s why:

low[v] ≥ disc[u]


👉 articulation point confirmed.

